Introduction
Imagine being able to send a letter to an ancestor living in January 1725 with knowledge of the next twenty-five years of history. Your ancestor has an important financial decision to make: whether to invest in the British East India Company (EIC) or the Dutch East India Company (VOC). Both companies are among the most powerful commercial organizations in the world, and both offer opportunities to profit from international trade. However, their economic circumstances, political relationships, business practices, and long-term prospects are different.
Looking back from 1750, the British East India Company represents the stronger investment opportunity. Although the Dutch East India Company remains a major commercial power, its organizational difficulties, declining competitiveness, and expensive military commitments weaken its position. The British company benefits from the growing strength of British international commerce and its expanding presence in India. For an investor seeking financial returns, the British East India Company is therefore the better choice. The recommended selling period is 1749, following the recovery of financial markets after the political and military disruptions of the mid-1740s. However, neither company represents an ethically responsible investment. Both accumulated wealth through colonial exploitation, monopolies, and coercive practices, although the Dutch company's particularly brutal history in the Spice Islands makes its ethical record especially troubling.
1. Understanding the Two Companies
The British East India Company was established on December 31, 1600, when Queen Elizabeth I granted a royal charter to a group of English merchants. Originally called the Governor and Company of Merchants of London Trading into the East Indies, the organization received exclusive trading privileges in regions beyond the Cape of Good Hope and the Strait of Magellan. Its original purpose was to establish profitable trading relationships with Asian markets, particularly in spices, textiles, and other valuable commodities.
