The company eventually developed into a powerful commercial and political organization. By 1725, it maintained trading settlements throughout India and other parts of Asia. Its principal commercial interests included cotton textiles, silk, pepper, and other commodities. Unlike an ordinary trading business, the EIC also possessed military capabilities and could negotiate with local rulers, maintain fortified settlements, and defend its commercial interests. Its influence would continue expanding throughout the eighteenth century.
The Dutch East India Company, known as the Vereenigde Oostindische Compagnie (VOC), was founded in 1602. It received a monopoly from the Dutch government over Dutch commercial activity east of the Cape of Good Hope and west of the Strait of Magellan. Its establishment united several competing Dutch trading organizations into one powerful corporation. The VOC developed an extensive trading network stretching from the Netherlands through southern Africa and across Asia.
The VOC's most important headquarters in Asia was Batavia, located in present-day Jakarta, Indonesia. From there, it coordinated commercial activities throughout the Indonesian archipelago, India, Japan, and other Asian markets. The company became particularly powerful through its control of the spice trade, especially nutmeg, mace, and cloves.
