Although both companies operated similarly, there were important organizational differences. The VOC had a more complicated administrative structure, consisting of six separate chambers governed by a central board known as the Gentlemen Seventeen. The British company operated through a more centralized structure based in London. Both companies also developed permanent joint-stock capital, allowing investors to purchase shares and trade them on established financial markets.
An important distinction concerned their financial policies. The VOC frequently retained earnings and relied heavily on borrowing to finance its expanding operations. The British company developed a stronger tradition of distributing cash returns to shareholders. These differences would influence the attractiveness of their shares to investors.
