2. The Financial Fortunes of Both Companies, 1725–1750
In January 1725, both companies were established commercial powers. However, their long-term prospects were not equally promising.
The VOC entered this period with considerable wealth and an established international trading network. Nevertheless, its financial position was becoming increasingly complicated. Its administrative structure created inefficiencies, while maintaining its overseas settlements and military presence required substantial expenditure. Competition from the British and other European trading companies also threatened its commercial dominance.
One particularly important problem was the company's reliance on monopolies. Although controlling the supply of valuable spices could generate substantial profits, maintaining those monopolies required expensive military and political intervention. The company also struggled with corruption and the financial consequences of maintaining its extensive overseas operations.
The British East India Company, meanwhile, was becoming increasingly competitive in Asian commerce. Its growing involvement in Indian textile markets and its commercial relationships with Indian rulers provided opportunities for expansion. British commercial influence was also supported by the increasing strength of Britain's maritime and financial systems.
The two companies' share prices provide an important way to understand their relative performance. Historian Larry Neal explains that historical price quotations are available for British East India Company shares from 1698 onward and Dutch East India Company shares from 1723 onward. The Dutch quotations were published in the Amsterdamsche Courant, while British quotations appeared in publications such as John Castaing's The Course of the Exchange. These records make it possible to compare the companies' financial fortunes during the period in question.
