The period also contained several important political and economic disruptions. The War of the Austrian Succession (1740–1748) created uncertainty throughout European financial markets. The conflict involved Britain, France, and other European powers, and their rivalries extended into Asian trading territories.
In 1746, French forces captured Madras, one of the British company's important settlements in India. This demonstrated the vulnerability of European trading establishments during wartime. However, the Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle, signed in 1748, ended the conflict and provided an opportunity for commercial and financial recovery. Madras was returned to British control under the settlement.
