The British company was not without financial risks. The loss of Madras in 1746 demonstrated that its overseas commercial network could be disrupted by military conflict. Nevertheless, its eventual recovery following the 1748 peace settlement made it a more attractive long-term investment than the increasingly troubled VOC.
4. When Should the Investor Cash Out?
The recommended cash-out year is 1749.
The years immediately preceding this decision were financially unstable. The Jacobite Rising of 1745 and the wider War of the Austrian Succession created uncertainty in British and European financial markets. Rather than selling during the initial disruption, an investor would benefit from waiting for the political situation to stabilize.
The Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle in October 1748 marked the end of the War of the Austrian Succession. The agreement restored peace between the major European powers and returned Madras to the British. This was particularly significant for the East India Company because it restored an important trading settlement and reduced the immediate danger of continued European military confrontation in India.
