The following year, 1749, offered an opportunity to benefit from the improved political and financial environment. The broader British financial market demonstrated a recovery after the instability of 1745–1748. For example, historical records of British government securities show that three-percent stock recovered from an annual low of 75 in 1746 to a high of 102 in 1749. Although these are government securities rather than East India Company shares, they demonstrate the broader recovery in British financial markets.
