This exploitation continued to produce enormous commercial advantages during the eighteenth century. Research on Banda's nutmeg economy indicates that in the mid-eighteenth century the VOC purchased nutmeg at approximately 1.125 stuivers per pound and sold it in the Dutch Republic for approximately 75 stuivers. This extraordinary difference demonstrates how much of the economic value was captured by the colonial monopoly rather than the local producers.
